{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Remember Abraham, Isaac, and Israel Your servants.</b> If Moses our master, master of all the prophets, needed to invoke the merit of the fathers in his prayer, how much more so do we need to invoke in prayer 'Elohei Avraham Elohei Yitzhak veElohei Yaakov' and to intend thereby that in this the Holy One, blessed be He, delights — in being mentioned in every generation." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> 'Remember Abraham, Isaac, and Israel' — Moses was not answered until he invoked the dead, and this is why Israel is likened to a vine, as it says (Psalms 80:9): ״גפן ממצרים תסיע״. Just as this vine lives and leans upon dead trees — and they are the wooden supports that elevate it upon them — so too Israel live and endure and lean upon the dead, who are the patriarchs." "<b>And so too</b> you find: Elijah prayed many prayers on Mount Carmel that fire descend from heaven, and that is what he said (I Kings 18:37): ״ענני ה׳ ענני״ — and he was not answered until he invoked the dead, as it says: ״ה׳ אלהי אברהם יצחק וישראל היום יודע כי יש אלהים בישראל״; immediately, ״ותפול אש ה׳ ותאכל את העולה״. So too Moses stood on the mountain forty days to seek mercy for Israel for the sin of the calf, and was not answered until he invoked the dead and said, 'Remember Abraham' — immediately, ״וינחם ה׳ על הרעה״. This is what Scripture says (Ecclesiastes 4:2): ״ושבח אני את המתים שכבר מתו״ — even from the standpoint of the living righteous, for the righteous are greater in their death than in their lifetime. Another interpretation: ״ושבח אני את המתים״ — the former dead, more than the living of now. Know this: the decrees of Moses endure, whereas the decrees of kings today do not endure — for Moses ordained many ordinances, such as that which the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: ״וידבר משה את מועדי ה׳ אל בני ישראל״ — Moses ordained for Israel that they should inquire and expound concerning the matter of the day. And so too we find that Ezra ordained many ordinances that are practiced today. Hence you may say: ״ושבח אני את המתים״." "<b>And they shall inherit it forever.</b> That they shall inherit it for generations; and even though they will be exiled from it, it shall not become an inheritance for any other people but for them alone, in the manner written in the promise to Abraham (Genesis 17:8): ״לאחזת עולם״. And furthermore, ״ונחלו לעולם״ may include that they shall inherit that world which corresponds to it [the Land], and if so, there is in the verse a promise for this world and for the world to come."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:06:22.857622345Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}